Following up on the earlier review concern about `jobCost`'s "read-only" scoring not actually being read-only: I traced it in the current pushed code and it's still open, then built and verified a fix in an isolated worktree (your branch untouched).

## The problem, confirmed in the current code

`jobCost`'s only guard checks `b->globalGradient[swimClass] == NULL` — that's the *building* gradient. It then calls `Map::resourceAvailable()` with no equivalent guard, and `resourceAvailable → getGradient → getResourceGradient` allocates a full map-sized array and runs a whole-map propagation the first time that (team, resource, swim class) triple is queried. `roundTripDistance()` — the fast path `jobCost` tries first — isn't exempt either, since it unconditionally calls `resourceAvailable()` internally. Separately, `buildingAvailable()` calls `buildingGradient()`, which rebuilds a *dirty* field once `DIRTY_REBUILD_TICKS` has elapsed — the null-check guard doesn't catch that case since the pointer is non-null, just stale.

Net effect: a comparison scan meant only to pick the best of several swap candidates (`Team::swapTask`/`swapInn`, up to `Unit::MAX_COUNT` candidates per call) can trigger a full-map gradient build purely from being looked at — even for a single fetcher with nobody to swap with, since `swapTask`'s own outer `jobCost(unit, a, r, &own)` call hits the same path.

## The fix

Added three genuinely side-effect-free probes that read whatever gradient already exists and return `false` otherwise — never allocating, never rebuilding a dirty field, never touching a cache-use timestamp:

- `Map::peekBuildingDistance` (counterpart to `buildingAvailable`)
- `Map::peekResourceDistance`, two overloads (counterpart to `resourceAvailable`)
- `Map::peekRoundTripDistance` (counterpart to `roundTripDistance`)

Swapped `jobCost`/`innCost` in `TeamStep.cpp` over to these. Real navigation code (hiring, actual walking) is untouched and keeps building/rebuilding fields exactly as before — only the candidate-comparison scan changed. A candidate whose field isn't built yet is simply skipped for that scan, same as today, just without the side effect.

## Verification

Followed the repo's "confirm the regression actually fails against the unpatched code" convention:

- Added `test/TaskSwapScoringHarness.cpp` (registered in `src/SConscript` + CI): one scenario proves a lone fetcher being scored for a swap must not spring a whole-map resource gradient into existence; a second proves a genuine beneficial swap still executes correctly with the new probes.
- Reverted just the `TeamStep.cpp` half of this patch, rebuilt: the new test **fails** exactly where expected. Restored it, rebuilt: **passes**.
- Re-ran the existing suite against the full fix, all green on macOS/arm64/clang: `InnSwapHarness`, `BuildingExpelHarness`, `ResourceFetchTargetHarness`, `RoundTripHungerGateHarness`, `HiringBucketHarness`, `FetchApportionmentHarness`, and all 187 CppUnit tests.

Doesn't touch the separate throughput-cost or Petri-benchmark questions from earlier — just this allocation-during-scoring issue. Full patch below if you want to pull it in directly (applies cleanly on top of the current branch tip, `1470aa4c6`):

